  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "If \\(\\sin x=3\\cos x,\\) then what is \\(\\sin x\\cos x?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac16\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac15\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac29\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac14\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac3{10}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The relation \\(\\sin x=3\\cos x\\) lets us write both the identity and the desired product using \\(\\cos^2x\\). Substituting into \\(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1\\) gives\n\\[\n9\\cos^2x+\\cos^2x=1,\n\\qquad \\cos^2x=\\frac1{10}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\sin x\\cos x=3\\cos^2x=\\frac3{10}.\n\\]\nNo sign choice is needed, so this works for every angle satisfying the condition. The answer is \\(\\frac3{10}\\), choice E.",
